# Line endings, said once, so that the bytes a release is built from do not depend on
# whose machine is building it.
#
# This is not tidiness, it is a release that was failing: `tools/build-release.sh`
# refuses to build when `gofmt -l` names a file, and on a Windows runner a checkout with
# the usual `core.autocrlf=true` gives every .go file a carriage return on the end of
# every line — which gofmt rewrites to LF and therefore reports as unformatted. The whole
# tree was named, and the release stopped, over a line ending nobody wrote.
#
# So text is text, and it is checked out as LF everywhere: Go's own formatting is the only
# formatting this project has, and it wants LF.
* text=auto eol=lf

# And things that are not text at all, so that nothing tries to normalise them: the
# flower's icon, the pictures of it, the chimes it plays, and the two files that are
# build output rather than source (the linker's object and the exe) are all ignored by
# git, but a rule here is cheaper than an explanation later.
*.ico binary
*.png binary
*.mp3 binary
*.wav binary
*.exe binary
*.syso binary
